347	<!-- Q7 -->
348	<div class="question-block" id="q7">
349	  <div class="q-num">Question 7</div>
350	  <div class="q-text">The parity test in <code>test_cpp_bridge.py</code> uses a tolerance of <code>1e-9</code>. Why not <code>0.0</code> (exact equality)?</div>
351	  <div class="options">
352	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>Because the C++ implementation deliberately approximates the result for speed</button>
353	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span>Because different evaluation orders and FMA instructions in C++ can produce results that differ from NumPy at the level of floating-point rounding, even when both implementations are correct</button>
354	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>Because the test converts between Python <code>float</code> and C++ <code>double</code>, introducing a fixed precision loss at the boundary</button>
355	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>Because NaN values cannot be compared with <code>==</code> in Python</button>
356	  </div>
357	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q7-fb-correct">Correct. Floating-point arithmetic is not associative: <code>(a+b)+c != a+(b+c)</code> in general. Different compilers, optimization flags (especially FMA), and evaluation orders can produce results that differ in the last few ULPs even for mathematically identical algorithms.</div>
358	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q7-fb-wrong">The reason is floating-point non-associativity. Different evaluation orders between C++ (possibly using FMA) and NumPy can cause ULP-level differences even when both algorithms are correct. <code>1e-9</code> is tight enough to catch logic bugs but tolerant of rounding differences.</div>
359	</div>
